  <!-- ===================== ABOUT ============================= -->
  <section class="band">
    <div class="band__head"><h2>About</h2></div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>The piece watches the room. When someone steps into frame, face tracking picks out the landmarks it needs and hands them to a game system, which assigns the viewer to a category and writes them into a growing file of everyone who has stood there before.</p>

      <p>Nothing about that judgment is explained to the viewer while it happens. You see yourself measured, you see the verdict, and you see the archive keep filling whether you agree with the reading or not. The work is about how ordinary that process has become, and how little say the person in front of the camera has in it.</p>

      <p>[One or two sentences on what you learned making it, or how viewers actually behaved with it. Gallery pieces read very differently on paper than in the room, and this is where you say what surprised you.]</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ===================== HOW IT RUNS =======================
       Keep this. It is the section that shows you can plan and
       execute, not just design. Cut it only if it runs long.
       ======================================================== -->
  <section class="band">
    <div class="band__head"><h2>How it runs</h2></div>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>A single camera feeds MediaPipe for face and landmark tracking. TouchDesigner takes that data, smooths it, and passes it over OSC to a Unity application that holds the sorting logic and the visual state. Unity returns the composite to be projected.</p>

      <p>The system was built to survive a full gallery day without anyone tending it. Tracking recovers on its own when the room empties or a viewer walks out of frame mid-read, the state resets between viewers so no one inherits the last person's record, and the whole chain comes up from a cold start in one step at opening.</p>

      <p>[If you had to solve anything specific for the space, say so here. Lighting that broke the tracking, a projector throw that forced a rebuild of the composition, a latency budget you had to hit. Concrete problems are the most persuasive thing on a page like this.]</p>
    </div>
  </section>

